e its own movements. In \"cancel culture,\" single transgressions define entire characters, stripping away context. Without nuance, justice devolves into retribution.\n\n\n**Illustrating with Examples**\n\nMy current focus is on illustrating the power of nuance through concrete examples. I\u0027m expanding on my supporting view regarding national governance. I\u0027m highlighting how Singapore successfully navigates complex issues like immigration, showing how the rejection of either closed or open border approach and its adoption of a system incorporating quotas demonstrates the necessity of gray areas for national well-being. I am developing this further by adding how this allows the government to adapt to ever-changing data. I am also working on another supporting view to cover Human Connection and Identity.\n\n\n**Concluding the Argument**\n\nI am now synthesizing my conclusions. I\u0027ve realized the retreat from nuance represents a defensive posture against overwhelming complexity. Binary thinking, while alluring in its simplicity, ultimately fractures society, so I will be refining my argument here. I need to make the claim that embracing nuance and ambiguity is actually a display of civic and moral maturity, vital for democratic institutions and shared humanity.\n\n\n",
